Use your own Linux machine as both the SSH client and SSH server. Everything can stay on 127.0.0.1.
1. Install the test components
On Ubuntu/Debian:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openssh-server openssh-client terminator autossh curl python3
sudo systemctl enable --now ssh
Confirm SSH is listening:
ss -ltn | grep ':22'
ssh "$USER"@127.0.0.1
Get ordinary localhost SSH working before testing the script. Ubuntu uses the openssh-server package, and recent releases may activate it through systemd socket activation. Ubuntu OpenSSH information
2. Optional: create a dedicated test key
This avoids entering your password separately in every pane:
install -d -m 700 ~/.ssh

ssh-keygen \
  -t ed25519 \
  -f ~/.ssh/tunnel-test \
  -N '' \
  -C 'localhost tunnel testing'

touch ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys

grep -qxF "$(cat ~/.ssh/tunnel-test.pub)" ~/.ssh/authorized_keys ||
  cat ~/.ssh/tunnel-test.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
Test it:
ssh -i ~/.ssh/tunnel-test "$USER"@127.0.0.1
Type exit after it connects.
3. Start a local test website
Open a separate terminal and run:
mkdir -p /tmp/tunnel-test
printf 'SSH tunnel test worked!\n' >/tmp/tunnel-test/index.html

python3 -m http.server 8000 \
  --bind 127.0.0.1 \
  --directory /tmp/tunnel-test
Leave this running. Verify it directly:
curl http://127.0.0.1:8000
4. Check the script’s dependencies
chmod +x ssh-tunnel-master.sh
./ssh-tunnel-master.sh --doctor
Then start it:
./ssh-tunnel-master.sh
5. Add a local tunnel
Choose 1 — Add local forward and enter:
Tunnel name: Local Web Test
SSH server: 127.0.0.1
SSH username: your Linux username
SSH port: 22
Identity file: ~/.ssh/tunnel-test
Jump host: leave blank
Compression: no
Automatic reconnect: no
Alive interval: 30
Alive count: 3
Local bind address: 127.0.0.1
Local listening port: 8080
Destination host: 127.0.0.1
Destination port: 8000
This produces the equivalent of:
ssh -N -T \
  -L 127.0.0.1:8080:127.0.0.1:8000 \
  -i ~/.ssh/tunnel-test \
  "$USER"@127.0.0.1
6. Add a remote tunnel
Choose 2 — Add remote forward:
Tunnel name: Remote Web Test
SSH server: 127.0.0.1
SSH username: your Linux username
SSH port: 22
Identity file: ~/.ssh/tunnel-test
Jump host: leave blank
Compression: no
Automatic reconnect: no
Remote bind address: 127.0.0.1
Remote listening port: 9000
Destination host: 127.0.0.1
Destination port: 8000
Because the SSH client and server are the same computer, port 9000 will also appear locally.
7. Add a SOCKS tunnel
Choose 3 — Add local SOCKS proxy:
Tunnel name: Local SOCKS Test
SSH server: 127.0.0.1
SSH username: your Linux username
SSH port: 22
Identity file: ~/.ssh/tunnel-test
Jump host: leave blank
Compression: no
Automatic reconnect: no
Local SOCKS bind address: 127.0.0.1
Local SOCKS listening port: 1080
OpenSSH supports local, remote, dynamic SOCKS, Unix-socket and tunnel-device forwarding. Official OpenSSH manual
8. Preview and launch
Select 6 to review the tunnels.
Select D first. This prints every SSH command without opening connections.
Restart the script or return to the menu, then select L. Terminator should open with three labeled panes:
- Local Web Test
- Remote Web Test
- Local SOCKS Test
The script uses Terminator’s DBus interface to make and label the splits. Terminator DBus documentation
9. Test each tunnel
In another terminal:
# Local -L tunnel
curl http://127.0.0.1:8080

# Remote -R tunnel
curl http://127.0.0.1:9000

# Dynamic -D SOCKS tunnel
curl --noproxy '' \
  --socks5-hostname 127.0.0.1:1080 \
  http://127.0.0.1:8000
Each command should print:
SSH tunnel test worked!
Check all listeners:
ss -ltn | grep -E ':(8000|8080|9000|1080)\b'
Stop a tunnel with Ctrl-C inside its Terminator pane. Stop the Python test server with Ctrl-C in its original terminal.
